    <semantic:process id="_387da2ea-97fe-4a87-a34b-d07b1ecf5831" name="Page 1" triso:readOnly="false" triso:collectEnds="true" isClosed="false">
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_a5fcc134-ce0a-4890-aebc-07997f514578">
            <semantic:text>use tokens as well</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_e99e7cb5-6981-42b6-9507-d0b986fa1773">
            <semantic:text>Today's concept: Sequence flow rules (intro). In a creator brand-collab request scenario, ask: What starts the flow, where can it split, how does it rejoin, and what ends it? Name tasks verb-object and avoid crossing lines. If a decision is needed, pick the gateway that matches the semantics. If data crosses pools, use a message flow, not a sequence flow. Keep one clear idea per diagram. Here's the one-sentence way to remember it: make it obvious, and test it with token animation if your tool supports it. Pause and sketch a 5-step example.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_bc96791d-2c82-4407-9f5b-9d2877cb9d9d">
            <semantic:text>Define Sequence flow rules (intro) in BPMN terms.
Identify when to apply Sequence flow rules (intro).
Model a 4-6 step example using Sequence flow rules (intro) correctly.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
    </semantic:process>
